WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.020
as this fiscal deficit whenever inflation going up and that's going to be also disastrous.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:19.040
Up next, Book TV presents Afterwards, an hour-long discussion with the author of a new book.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.920
This week, former CIA officer John Carriacou talks about his service in the agency, both

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.840
before and after 9-11.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.740
He played a key role in the capture of al-Qaeda associate Abu Zubaydah in Pakistan and

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.220
also aided U.S. officials with the planning of the Iraq War.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.840
He's interviewed by Frederick Hitts, former Inspector General of the CIA and current senior

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:52.790
fellow at the University of Virginia's Center for National Security Law.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:01:00.430
Sitting here with John Carriacou, talking about his new book, The Reluctant Spy, I told

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:06.430
John before we got on the air that I'd like to ask him as my first question, how did

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.590
you decide to write this book, John?

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.470
It was a hard decision, actually.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.870
I love telling stories.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:19.670
I've been told that I'm a good storyteller, but I was a little bit reluctant to begin because

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.630
I was intimidated by the review process, the CIA's publications review process.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.190
And then I thought, so many of my friends have written books, former colleagues from

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:33.030
the agency, and I thought that George Tennant's book really broke new ground in what could

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.430
be released, so I thought I'd give it a try.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.030
And what I wanted to convey, really, was how hard the CIA works to keep the

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.230
country safe.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:46.190
So much is written about the agency's failures, just because by their nature failures

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.750
are what is generally released to the public.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:53.070
And I wanted to try to write a story about the agency's successes.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:57.030
I think I'm also, I've enjoyed very much reading this book.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:03.190
I think how you came to go into the agency was very interesting.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:06.190
Could you talk a little bit about how you made that decision?

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.190
Sure.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.590
I hadn't even been considering the agency as a career.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:14.790
I was in graduate school at George Washington University here in Washington, D.C.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:21.230
And I was taking a class called the Psychology of Leadership with an eminent professor, Dr.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.590
Gerald Post.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.390
And Dr. Post asked me to stay after class one day.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.510
He wanted to talk, I thought he wanted to talk about a paper that I had written.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.910
So I stayed after class and he said, listen, do you have any plans for what you're

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.630
going to do after graduation?

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:41.230
And I said, oh, Dr. Post, not only do I not have a job, I don't even have any prospects

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.110
for a job and I'm getting married just a few weeks after I graduate and I don't know

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.790
what I'm going to do.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:49.430
And he said, well, would you consider the CIA?

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.470
And I thought, sure, that sounds interesting.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.450
I would consider the CIA.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:54.910
So we walked down to his office.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.550
He called a man who to this day I know only as Bob.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:03.710
I think Bob was the director of personnel for the agency at the time.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.830
This is in the late 1980s.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:12.670
And 30 minutes later, I'm in a nondescript office in suburban Virginia speaking with Bob.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:16.670
It began a series of tests the following Saturday.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:17.670
And it was a long process.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.790
It took about 14 months in total.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.430
But 14 months later I started working at the agency.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:28.390
Were you aware at the time or did you come to be aware of the fact that this

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:37.510
particular technique, one of your professors or an older person known to you, might be a

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.590
spotter?

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:45.910
That is, was there any legend about that around GW that there was a CIA sort of recruiter on

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:46.910
the faculty?

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:47.910
No.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.590
It was kept quiet, I think.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.870
And it's funny because in retrospect it was so clear what he was doing.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:00.430
He was acting as a spotter looking for grad students who he thought would be a good fit

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.430
in the agency.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:07.750
It turned out that Dr. Post was really a legendary CIA officer on the analytic side and someone

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.470
who has had a lasting positive effect on the agency.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.750
So I was really very fortunate not just to have been in a position where he could

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.750
help me, but really to have known him and studied under him.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.750
It's something that really benefited me in my career.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:27.910
And following on that, it's much more impressive, isn't it, when you think that one of your teachers

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:33.670
or one of your mentors comes to you with a suggestion that you look at this, and I think

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:40.470
the agency over the years has had a lot of success with those persons in various faculties

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:47.230
and institutions who have been on the lookout for able young people.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.510
And it's certainly a compliment to you when you thought about it, etc.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.070
I was very flattered.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:57.230
And then I came to meet a great number of people inside the agency once I was hired who had made

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.710
it through the process in exactly the same way.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:04.950
And then interestingly enough, when I finally got hired I went to thank Dr. Post and he

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:11.190
denied having anything to do with my success in the process other than introducing me to

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.270
a CIA personnel person who could get the process going.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.190
He really insisted that he had nothing to do with it.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:19.630
And I knew that wasn't really true.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.230
He was extremely helpful to me.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:27.510
What was your decision process, John, in trying to figure out which part of the agency to

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.510
go into?

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.510
I went into the process pretty naive.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.110
I mean, I of course knew that there was a directorate of operations.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.230
There was a directorate of intelligence.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.590
There were two other directorates that weren't really a fit for me.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:43.990
But I was offered interviews in both operations and intelligence.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.830
And I had discussed this with my wife at the time.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:51.990
And she really did not want to live most of our adult lives overseas.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:57.270
So in that initial interview with the directorate of operations, they asked me, well, what would

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:02.430
your wife say about living in Sudan or some other third or fourth world country?

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.430
And I said, oh, she wouldn't want to do that at all.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.950
And the man stood up and put out his hand and said, well, thanks for coming today.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.070
So I knew that that hadn't gone very well.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:14.390
Well, no, it had gone fine because you got to the point that was relevant.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:15.390
That's right.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:20.510
But you weren't going to be a happy person with your wife unhappy and as well get that

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:21.810
sorted out.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.750
So you made the decision that you wanted to do the analytical side.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:32.350
Did you have any regions or issues in mind coming in that you would intrigue you more

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:33.350
than others?

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.350
I did.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.350
I had a bachelor's degree in Middle Eastern studies at the time.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.710
And I was absolutely fascinated by the Middle East.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:46.510
When I was in high school, the Iranian government collapsed, the government of the Shah collapsed,

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:51.150
and the Aitola Khomeini took power, of course, American hostages were seized, and I was captivated

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:57.310
by the whole 444-day saga of what had happened to our diplomats there.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.910
And so I decided early on, really in high school, that I wanted to do something related

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.310
to the Middle East.

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.710
And I was willing to learn Arabic.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:09.030
My focus as an undergrad was in Islamic theology.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:12.910
I really wanted to have an understanding of what we were up against.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.670
And so it's funny.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:19.790
When I first interviewed on the intelligence side, I was told that I was being considered

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:24.470
for two positions, one on Iraq and Kuwait and the other on North Korea.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.390
And I said, but I don't know anything about North Korea.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:28.390
And they said, that's okay.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.550
We can train you.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.630
So I was very fortunate in the end that not only was I hired for Iraq and Kuwait,

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:38.310
but I was hired to work in the office that Dr. Post had founded, which was no

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:43.030
longer exists, but it was involved in the analysis of motivations of foreign leaders.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.430
Motivations of foreign leaders.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:45.430
Yeah, very interesting.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.270
And at the time, unique agency concern.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:55.950
But back to this question of Iraq, Kuwait, it would have been a waste of your talent

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:01.790
and your background if you'd been pitched over to North Korea, even though they doubtless

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:04.390
could have gotten you started there.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:05.390
I thought so too.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:11.310
And I was told on my very first day when they gave me my assignment that I shouldn't be disheartened

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.310
by this assignment.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.310
Of course I wasn't.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.310
I was very excited.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.590
But I shouldn't be disheartened because this was what they called a training account.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.550
They said nothing ever happens in these countries.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:27.670
There's a nascent democracy movement in Kuwait that's moderately interesting.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:33.030
And in Iraq, it's the same cabinet since the 1968 revolution.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.030
Nothing ever happens.

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:35.670
Wrong.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:36.670
Wrong.

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:38.310
Learned the CIA writing style.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.470
And you can do something exciting like Romania.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:41.470
I love it.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.750
And I thought, well, okay.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.550
And then we got to the point where about nine months later, just as I felt like I knew

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.270
what I was doing, Iraq invaded Kuwait.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.270
And then a senior analyst came up to me and said, look, you have a unique opportunity

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:54.270
here.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.270
He said, it's not unusual for the countries we cover to go to war.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.710
But it is unusual for the countries we cover to go to war with us.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:02.950
So you really have to make something out of this.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:06.390
And I worked as hard as I could and became the go-to guy on Iraq.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.830
It was a very good piece of information or piece of advice.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.310
I'm interested in your GW prep.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:20.590
Did you consider taking or studying Arabic at that time or any of the languages related

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:21.590
to the Middle East?

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:22.590
I did.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.390
I had a problem though in that it wasn't, Arabic wasn't offered at GW.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:32.350
GW at the time didn't have a strong language component to their regional studies programs.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:39.750
In fact, I had a lot of trouble even finding a class on Islam to take.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:45.390
They were more focused on oil, oil security, the history of relations of the region, things

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.470
like that, the politics of North Africa, for example.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:52.630
So I went out and found courses on Islam to take.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:54.390
Some of them were at the grad level.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:59.470
Some were called experimental classes where they hired the former Shah of Iran's

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.310
chief of staff to come from Philadelphia.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:06.350
He was teaching at Temple and he would come once a week and teach a class.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:11.430
So I applied for those and was accepted for those.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:18.030
And then even courses on religion, for example, I had to go out and find them.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:23.190
The New Testament, the Hebrew Scriptures, these Islamic theology courses, I really

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:25.190
had to go out and look for them.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:26.350
Yeah, interesting.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.590
And on the language business.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:29.950
Now, you had spoken some Greek at home.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:30.950
Right.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:31.950
I spoke some Greek at home.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.550
So you knew that you could handle languages.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.590
I kind of had a facility for languages.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:39.670
I actually had a little bit of trouble when I got to the university level with Greek

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.590
because we had spoken Greek at home.

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:42.590
Of course.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:48.270
But then when I went to study it formally at school, I was told that I spoke Greek

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:55.070
at in 1930 era slang, which was, of course, what my grandparents had taught my parents

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:56.950
and my parents passed on to me.

00:10:56.950 --> 00:11:03.230
Me never having been to Greece and not realizing I was speaking in 50, 60-year-old slang.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.630
So I had to relearn Greek from the very beginning.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:08.470
And then at the agency, they offered me Arabic and I jumped at the chance.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:10.070
Well, that's the thing that interests me.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.190
So you clearly had a good ear.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:15.630
Now what about the issue in terms of incentives?

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:21.670
In other words, the agency offered you an opportunity to study Arabic.

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:28.590
Was there any encouragement in terms of a stipend because that's a lot of work?

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:29.590
It was a lot of work.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:34.670
Could you earn a little bit extra salary if you really poured on the coal and learned

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:35.670
it well?

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:36.670
You could.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:37.670
And it was a nice incentive.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:43.950
It was something like a five-year incentive at $3,000 a year to the best of my recollection.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.910
I wanted to do well just because I wanted to do well and I always had it in the back

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:52.670
of my mind that I should have really sought out Arabic in college and I didn't.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:54.230
I took the easier way out.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:55.230
That was a mistake.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:12:01.150
Well, you know, not to make you take the pressure off you because I know you believe

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:08.350
that in good conscience, but I found in college teaching, combining a hard language like

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:13.510
that with a full program of other substantive studies, it is hard.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:14.510
It is really difficult.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:19.110
At the end of my Arabic training at the agency, I told my Arabic teacher, I feel like you owe

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:23.350
me a master's degree because I never worked this hard in graduate school.

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:24.790
It's interesting.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:30.510
And then once you had acquired the language, that opened up additional possibilities

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:31.510
for your career.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:32.510
It did.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:36.550
I ended up transferring temporarily to the State Department.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:41.630
There was a unique program that was offered in the Director of Intelligence at the time

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:46.450
that allowed CIA analysts to do a two-year rotation to the State Department just to get

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:49.470
an idea of what it's like to work in the policy community.

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:54.030
So I did that and I became an economic officer at the American Embassy in Bahrain for two

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.030
years.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:59.070
And I have to say they were two of the most enjoyable years of my career.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:06.230
I really loved living and working in the field, doing real policy work, accompanying

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:10.110
the ambassador to meetings with high-level leaders.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:12.250
It was really the right thing to do.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.990
And was that an accompanied tour for you and your family?

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:16.990
It was.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:21.310
And my wife really went kicking and screaming.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:25.950
And it was funny because the deal that I made with her was I said, just give it six

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:30.230
weeks and if you're truly unhappy at the end of six weeks, I'll send you home

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:31.810
and you can wait for me there.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:37.350
So we got there and as soon as we arrived, she saw the enormous house they put us

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:38.350
in.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:44.470
State of the art shopping malls, wonderful four-star restaurants, and then we very quickly

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:45.910
made friends.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:50.310
And at the end of my two years, she asked me about the possibility of extending for

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:51.390
a third.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:55.350
At the time it was just a two-year tour, so I wasn't allowed to extend, but I would

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:56.550
have in a heartbeat.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:14:00.070
But that developed in you the taste for overseas service?

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:04.550
I had the travel bug by then, and I really wanted to serve overseas again.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:09.790
But again, at the time, an analyst was only really allowed to do two years in that

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:11.830
analyst overseas program.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:16.150
You had to be much more senior in your career to go overseas on a permanent

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:17.550
basis again.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.270
So it was going to be many years before I had the opportunity.

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:23.750
But was that when you started to get a little itchy feet and started looking

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:27.630
for possibilities to get involved with the operational end?

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.830
Oh, it was indeed.

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.910
By the time I decided to switch over to operations, I had been

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:38.590
working almost exclusively on Iraq for seven and a half years.

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:43.630
And early on in my career, I told myself, just sit tight on Iraq until Saddam Hussein

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.030
is overthrown, and then you can move on and do something different.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:50.350
I thought maybe the peace process or something interesting.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:55.470
But by 1997, it was clear Saddam Hussein wasn't going anywhere.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:58.190
And I had ants in my pants to travel again.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:04.510
And a job opened up that would have allowed me to go to Athens, Greece with real

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:07.470
regularity to spend a great deal of time there.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:08.950
And it was in counter-terrorism.

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.230
But I didn't have any operational experience whatsoever.

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:16.110
So I went down to the CIA's counter-terrorist center, and I talked with

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:19.350
the senior officer who was in charge of hiring for the position.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:22.590
And I told him I don't have any operational experience at all, but I

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:26.390
speak both Greek and Arabic, and his mouth dropped open.

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:28.830
And he said, are you willing to be tested?

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:33.710
And I said, sure, I just tested recently in Arabic, but I'll test again if you want.

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:38.390
What turned out his secretary was born and raised on the same island that my grandparents

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:39.750
came from in Greece.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:45.150
So she came out of the office and began asking me questions in Greek, to which I

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.870
responded in Greek.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.910
And she turned and she says, he gets the thumbs up from me.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:55.230
So my Arabic scores were current, and he told me it's going to take some convincing

00:15:55.230 --> 00:16:02.190
on my part to get the higher-ups to believe that we should put an analyst in this operational

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:03.190
position.

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:07.510
But he said it's a lot easier and a lot cheaper to take a linguist and teach him

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:12.110
operations than to take an operations person and teach him how to speak Greek and Arabic.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:19.110
At the time, did that comment give you cause for thought?

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:23.070
It's an interesting point, because the languages are tough.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:26.190
And I thought, do I really understand what I'm getting into here?

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:27.190
I think I do.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:31.190
But then when I went back to my office to tell my boss that I decided to go forward

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:36.190
with this application, he said, you know, this is going to be a complete life change.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:37.750
There's no turning back here.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:39.750
Once you make this decision, this is it.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.750
And I said, I know what I'm getting into.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.750
I didn't, in retrospect.

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:45.750
But I'm glad I made the decision.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:50.030
But the point is that for people contemplating a career like this, this

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:52.150
represents, as you say, a life-changing experience.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:53.150
It really is.

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:58.150
And you've just articulated the fact that your wife had reservations about going to

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.150
the hell holes of the world.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:01.150
Right.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.150
Not that Greece was.

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:03.150
I didn't mean that.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:08.150
But once you turn your compass in that direction, you know, not every assignment's

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.150
going to be Athens.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.150
That's right.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.150
It's funny, too, because she told me at the time the only place that she

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:15.150
would want to go would be Athens.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.150
If I could get Athens, I would go to Athens.

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.150
If I could get Athens, great.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:24.150
And then I thought, well, I'll do a good job in Athens.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.150
Maybe I can go to another European country.

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.150
Or I loved the Middle East.

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:30.150
Maybe I can go to one of the really great Middle Eastern countries.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:37.150
Not thinking I'm going to end up in Baghdad or Kabul or Islamabad at some point in the future.

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:39.150
It just never entered my mind.

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:43.150
And I thought, well, I'll just take it one day at a time and see where this leads me.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.150
So I applied and got the job.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:46.150
That's terrific.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:50.150
Well, tell us a little bit about that, that first job over there.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:54.150
I really didn't know what to expect.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.150
I should have.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:59.150
I should have at least had an idea, because here I was going out as the representative of the CIA's

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:01.150
Counterterrorist Center.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:04.150
So I would be working on counterterrorism issues.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:12.150
About which would you have said we in the agency knew a lot, a little, not as much as we would like to?

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:16.150
I think we knew a lot on the issues that I was going to cover.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:21.150
I went to cover Greek terrorism, which believe it or not, before September 11th, the U.S.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:29.150
government spent more money on security in Athens than it did in any other capital city in the world, including Beirut.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:36.150
People really didn't have an appreciation at the time for how dangerous Athens was for Americans, official Americans.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:37.150
And why was that?

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:40.150
Because you go into that in your book and it's fascinating.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.150
There were two groups at the time who were killing Americans.

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:55.150
One was called Revolutionary Organization 17 November, which was named after a day in 1973 when the military dictatorship used tanks to crush a peaceful student rally.

00:18:55.150 --> 00:19:00.150
And the other was called Popular Revolutionary Struggle.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:08.150
Popular Revolutionary Struggle, or ELA, it was called as its Greek acronym, mostly targeted Greek policemen and Greek government officials.

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:12.150
They also went after U.S. military personnel.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:18.150
Usually low-level people, they would blow up buses or put bombs in cars on the side of the road, things like that.

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:21.150
But 17 November was much, much more lethal.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:25.150
They would do close-in handgun assassinations for the most part.

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:28.150
They did use some rockets and some bombs as well.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:37.150
As you point out, they had been responsible for the assassination of a very much-beloved and capable CIA station chief.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:44.150
Yes, Richard Welch was assassinated in 1975 on the 23rd of December.

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:49.150
And the CIA promised Mrs. Welch that we would bring his killers to justice.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:57.150
Believe me when I say that all of us in the 17 November unit at headquarters really took that very seriously and very personally.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:03.150
And we dedicated our lives to cracking this group and bringing these guys to justice.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:08.150
To the point where we even admitted to each other that we were obsessed with 17 November.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:11.150
I was proud to go to Greece and work against this group.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:13.150
Now it wasn't just 17 November.

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:19.150
Practically every Arab terrorist group in the world was represented in Athens as well.

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:24.150
Palestinians, Syrians, Libyans, Iranians, well Iranians obviously aren't Arabs,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:28.150
but every bad guy seemed to have had some sort of presence in Athens.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:32.150
And so it kept us very busy from my very first day as a matter of fact.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:38.150
So you were really getting in on the ground floor before the balloon went up on 9-11.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:46.150
You were getting a real exposure to big time kind of terrorist activity.

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:55.150
I was and in retrospect it really prepared me well for the post-September 11th world.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:57.150
I had great teachers in Athens too.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:04.150
We had an absolutely fantastic group of people working in and out of Athens,

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.150
at least working on Greek issues.

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:08.150
And I learned a great deal from those men.

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:18.150
Well take us into this run up to 9-11 and then to some of the responsibilities you acquired after that.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:24.150
When I returned from Athens I went to work again on Greek issues.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:32.150
There used to be a group that was focused solely on 17 November within the DCI's counter-terrorist center.

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:38.150
So on the morning of September 11th I was supposed to go to the White House with Kofor Black,

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:43.150
another legendary officer who at the time was the director of the counter-terrorist center, CTC.

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:48.150
Kofor and I were supposed to go to the White House to speak with Condi Rice about a Greek issue.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:55.150
And I was walking over to his office to tell him that the car was ready to take us downtown.

00:21:55.150 --> 00:22:02.150
And as I passed his secretary's desk I noticed that she had her TV on and that one of the World Trade Center towers was burning.

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:05.150
And I said, oh, what happened to the World Trade Center?

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:11.150
And she said, an airplane flew into it and me not realizing what in the world was happening,

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:17.150
I said, oh you know that happened once before in the 1930s an airplane hit the Empire State Building.

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:21.150
And it was very foggy that day. And I said, it's funny, it's so clear today.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:24.150
How could the pilot not see that he's flying into the World Trade Center?

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:28.150
And just after I said that, the second plane flew into tower one.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:34.150
And it was such a surreal thing that neither she nor I reacted to it.

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:37.150
And then finally she turned and said, did you see that?

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:40.150
And I immediately ran back to my office.

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:45.150
And I said, guys, I think we're under attack, two planes just flew into the World Trade Center.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:49.150
Everybody ran to the front office where Cofer's office was located.

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:51.150
By then we had turned on TVs everywhere.

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:56.150
And after a few minutes, many more than a hundred people had gathered.

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:59.150
And we're standing there in silence.

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.150
Finally the third plane hits the Pentagon.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:05.150
And somebody behind me shouted, will somebody please lead?

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:08.150
I thought that was a terrific part of the book.

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:16.150
And if you read a lot of the collateral accounts of what were people in different positions

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:22.150
in and around Washington, much the same story could be told that people were just stunned.

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:28.150
Things were happening at such a rapid clip that we'd all been told that this was a possibility.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.150
But here it was in real time.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:35.150
And we didn't quite know immediately what to do.

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:37.150
I go into this in the book too, and I think this is important.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:41.150
I got my first inkling that something was happening.

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:46.150
Because as I said, again, I was working on Greek terrorism, and I wasn't at all focused on Al Qaeda.

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:53.150
But on July the 6th, 2001, I was leading a group of Arab intelligence officers in the building.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.150
We do this all the time.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:55.150
It's good for relations.

00:23:55.150 --> 00:23:59.150
You do a meet and greet with a director or with a senior officer.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:02.150
You take them to the gift shop and get them some CIA souvenirs.

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:03.150
You do a couple of briefings.

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.150
It's good for relations.

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:10.150
And on this day I had asked that Kofor come just to shake their hands and thank them for coming.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.150
And he was very generous in that way.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:17.150
But that morning he came and he actually sat down, which was a surprise to me.

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:21.150
And he told them in no uncertain terms.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:23.150
He said something terrible is going to happen.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:26.150
We don't know where, we don't know when.

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:30.150
But it's going to be an attack on a scale that we've never seen before.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:33.150
He said the mood in the camps is one of jubilation.

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:41.150
We're seeing camp commanders talking to their students and crying on the phone and saying,

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:43.150
I'll see you in paradise.

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:50.150
We're picking up code words about a massive wedding or a massive soccer match.

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:52.150
And these are all code words for an attack.

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:57.150
So he leans forward and he says, I beg you, if you have any sources inside Al Qaeda,

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.150
please help us disrupt this attack.

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.150
Well, it was a stunning briefing, chilling.

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:07.150
And at the end of the day I went back to his office to thank him for taking the time.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:11.150
And I said, Kofor, I have to ask you, was that for their benefit or were you serious?

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:14.150
And he said, oh, I was very serious.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:17.150
He said, Dick Clark and I, Dick Clark was at the National Security Council.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:20.150
Dick Clark and I have been shouting this from the rooftops.

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:22.150
And no one will pay attention to us.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:24.150
But something terrible is going to happen.

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:28.150
And then sure enough, two months later, there was September 11th.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:31.150
And that comes out in Dick Clark's book.

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:38.150
It comes out in the 9-11 commission report as they were assessing the tidbits that were coming in.

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:43.150
George Tenet with his hair on fire, remember that analogy?

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:50.150
Knowing something was going to happen, but not having the time, date, place kind of thing.

00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:53.150
Hardest part of information to get.

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:54.150
It really is.

00:25:54.150 --> 00:26:03.150
And I think also that people inside the agency had convinced themselves that the attack wouldn't be inside the United States.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:09.150
I think that we believed at the time they didn't have the wherewithal to attack us here domestically.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:24.150
So then you became everybody rallying round after the event to figure out what does one have to do to stop the possibility of a follow-on attack?

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:25.150
Right.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:30.150
Like everybody else in the building, I volunteered to go to Afghanistan.

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:35.150
Of course, they were only taking people with special forces experience at the time.

00:26:35.150 --> 00:26:37.150
And I had come from the analytic side.

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:41.150
I didn't have a lot of counter-terrorist experience, but I had Arabic.

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:44.150
And I thought at least I could go serve as a translator.

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:47.150
So I volunteered over and over and over again.

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:54.150
Finally, after a couple of months had passed, I went to the deputy director of CTC who was a friend of mine from the director of intelligence.

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.150
And I made a completely empty threat.

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:05.150
And I told him, if you don't send me over there, I'm going straight to Exxon with my Arabic and I'm never looking back.

00:27:05.150 --> 00:27:09.150
And he said, fine, all right, all right, we'll send you somewhere. Just give me a day.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:14.150
So the next day he called and he said, can you go to Pakistan as the chief of counter-terrorism operations there?

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:15.150
And I said, done.

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.150
Big job.

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:17.150
Big job.

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:18.150
Big job.

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:20.150
And it's funny, I didn't realize how big it was at the time.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:21.150
No.

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:22.150
But I just wanted to go.

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:24.150
I just wanted to serve and do something.

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:25.150
So the next day I left.

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:31.150
That makes the point that you make often in the book that the culture of the agency is let's do it.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:32.150
Let's do it.

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:34.150
This isn't unique to me.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:36.150
Everybody in the CIA was like this.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.150
We all wanted to do something.

00:27:38.150 --> 00:27:39.150
We all wanted to serve.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:43.150
We all wanted to go over there and contribute in any way that we could.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:48.150
Well, John, this is a, I think we're coming towards a moment where we have a little bit of a break.

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:55.150
But let's get into, in greater depth, what you found when you took up that assignment.

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:56.150
Terrific.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:28:01.980
Look forward to it.

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:07.980
Afterwards, and several other C-SPAN programs are available for download as podcasts.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:11.980
More with John Kiriakou and Frederick Hitz in a moment.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:21.660
Every weekend, C-SPAN 2's book TV features 48 hours of nonfiction books.

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:25.660
This weekend on afterwards, John Kiriakou is the reluctant spy.

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:30.660
The former CIA officer talks about life in the agency before and after 9-11.

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:34.660
He's interviewed by former CIA Inspector General Frederick Hitz.

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:38.660
Find the entire weekend schedule at booktv.org.

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:45.490
Afterwards with John Kiriakou and Frederick Hitz continues.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:58.300
John, you have a very interesting account of the takedown of the Taliban embassy in Peshawar in Pakistan.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:04.300
Talk a little bit about that because that will be, you know, it was news to me.

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:08.300
There would be such a thing that you could call it an embassy.

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:13.300
It's funny because embassies are almost always in capital cities.

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:20.300
This embassy happened to be in Peshawar, which is a town or a city in the northwest frontier province of Pakistan.

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:25.300
We had operational reason to go there every once in a while to see what was going on.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:30.300
It's kind of a wild west type of border city and very dangerous.

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:36.300
I happened to be with a colleague from the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:38.300
by the name of Detective Tommy McHale.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.300
Tommy was on loan at the time to the FBI.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:44.300
I admired him greatly.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:49.300
He is a true American hero, an unsung hero.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:53.300
He was a victim in the first World Trade Center attack.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:57.300
It damaged his lungs and he never fully recovered from that attack.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:03.300
Then his best friend was killed in the September 11th attack on the World Trade Center.

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:10.300
Tommy had his best friend's handcuffs and whenever we would do raid or capture an al-Qaeda fighter,

00:30:10.300 --> 00:30:15.300
he would cuff him with his best friend's cuffs and say that this was for his friend.

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:18.300
It was very emotional and it really made an impact on me.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:20.300
I thought it was very important.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:25.300
We were in Peshawar together and we happened to drive by the Taliban embassy,

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:27.300
which was still functioning.

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:34.300
This is in March or April of 2002, so it's well after the Taliban government had fallen in Afghanistan.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:36.300
It didn't make any sense to us.

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:39.300
As we were driving by, he pointed it out to me and he said,

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:41.300
you know what we should do?

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:45.300
We should break in there one night and just steal everything they have.

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.300
I laughed and I said, yeah, that would be funny.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:50.300
Then we looked at each other and I said, you know what?

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:53.300
That's exactly what we should do.

00:30:53.300 --> 00:31:02.300
We went back to Islamabad and Tommy pitched it to his FBI bosses and they gave the go ahead.

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:10.300
He requisitioned two vans from the embassy motor pool and assembled a team of people.

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:14.300
We drove up to Peshawar, waited until about two o'clock in the morning,

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:21.300
broke the cheap lock off the front door and went in and just started taking absolutely everything in the building.

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:26.300
Computers, files, cell phones, weapons, money, anything.

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:33.300
Anything that wasn't bolted down to the floor, we jammed it into these two vans and drove back to Islamabad.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:39.300
Well, because September 11th was an open criminal investigation, of course the FBI had the lead here.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:47.300
So Tommy, not emailed, but cabled the FBI saying, we did it, no problems, everybody's accounted for,

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:49.300
got everything, we're going to start going through it.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:53.300
They said, congratulations, please advise if you find anything interesting.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:58.300
Well four or five days later, he came up to my office and he said, you're never going to believe what I found.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:05.300
It was a file folder with telephone bills in it and the telephone bills were written in English.

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:15.300
They were Pakistani issued telephone bills and they documented 168 calls made from the Taliban embassy

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:18.300
to numbers inside the United States.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:25.300
And I mean all over the United States, Bethesda, Maryland, Los Angeles, Buffalo, Kansas City, all over the country.

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:36.300
And those calls stopped abruptly on September the 10th, 2001 and then started up again slowly on September the 16th.

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:39.300
So you had a live one?

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:46.300
A live one. This is the best lead that we had during the time that I was in Pakistan.

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:51.300
Well he was very, very excited about this. This was a very serious catch.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:56.300
And so he cabled the FBI and said, be advised, this is what we found.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:33:02.300
We're pouching the originals back. They said, good catch, please make sure the originals come to such and such an office.

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:06.300
And we were so busy I kind of put it out of my mind.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:13.300
So went back to the agency several months later, stayed in touch with Tommy,

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:18.300
and I asked him whatever happened to those phone numbers at one point.

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:23.300
He said nothing that I know of. I know that we sent them back to FBI headquarters.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.300
I know that they received them, but I haven't heard any follow-up.

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:33.300
Then a couple of years later I left the agency and I spoke to him again in 2005 and I asked him

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:37.300
whatever happened to those numbers, were the traces ever done?

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:40.300
And he said, you know, the FBI never traced those numbers.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:48.300
They said initially that they didn't have the Pashto translators necessary to dedicate to the job.

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:51.300
And I said, but the bills were in English.

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:54.300
They didn't need to be translated from Pashto.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:56.300
That was the reason why we knew what they were.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:59.300
And he said, well, all I know is what the FBI is talking about.

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:01.300
They were to parties in the United States.

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:03.300
They were to parties in the United States.

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:07.300
Now in a worst case scenario, this is terrorists living among us.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:08.300
Quite.

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:09.300
Or terrorists.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:13.300
Which we, it was clear that, and the bureau remember warned us about that.

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:14.300
Indeed.

00:34:14.300 --> 00:34:15.300
After 9-11.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:16.300
They did.

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:17.300
They could have sleeper cells.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:18.300
Sleeper cells.

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:23.300
Attorney General Ashcroft at the time was very clear about the possibility of sleeper cells in the United States.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:28.300
And Al-Qaeda's leadership had alluded to the fact that there were sleeper cells in the United States.

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:31.300
Well, 2007 finally comes.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:36.300
And I asked him once again, whatever happened to those numbers and the FBI traces?

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:40.300
He said that to the best of his knowledge, nothing was ever done with them.

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:47.300
And then finally I ran into an FBI, a former FBI colleague with whom I had served in Islamabad.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:55.300
And he said that the phone bills were put into a box and sent into an FBI storage facility in suburban Maryland.

00:34:55.300 --> 00:35:00.300
The box had never been opened and nobody had ever traced those calls.

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:05.300
So for all we know, here we are eight years later, those people are still living in the United States.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:13.300
And remember when we went into that complex NSA operation in the beginning of the George W. Bush administration

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:17.300
we were trying to get just that information.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:21.300
Who was Al-Qaeda calling in the United States and why?

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:23.300
That's right. And there it was in black and white.

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:25.300
And you never did anything with it.

00:35:25.300 --> 00:35:35.300
Well, one of the really most exciting parts of your book is your capture of Zubeida, this high-ranking Al-Qaeda official.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.300
Can you take us through that a little bit?

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:38.300
Sure.

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:44.300
In February of 2002 we got word from headquarters that Abu Zubedah was somewhere in Pakistan.

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:47.300
And that's all they said. He's somewhere in Pakistan.

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:51.300
Did you know him by that name at the time? Did you know what he was?

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:55.300
We did. We thought at the time that he was the number three ranking person in Al-Qaeda.

00:35:55.300 --> 00:36:01.300
That turned out to not exactly be true because at the time we didn't realize that Khalid Sheikh Mohammed was in Al-Qaeda

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:04.300
or that he had pledged fidelity to Osama bin Laden.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:11.300
So, Abu Zubedah was probably a little lower ranking, fourth or fifth, but still within the leadership.

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:17.300
We knew that he was the commander of the larger of Al-Qaeda's two main training camps in Afghanistan.

00:36:17.300 --> 00:36:19.300
We knew that he was a logistics whiz.

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:28.300
And by that I mean other Al-Qaeda fighters described him as the hub of the wheel from which all the spokes go out.

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:32.300
He knew everybody. He was the middleman.

00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:38.300
And he knew bin Laden. We suspected at the time that he may have had something to do with the September 11 attacks.

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:41.300
I think in the end he wasn't directly involved.

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:46.300
But he certainly was aware of Al-Qaeda's operations and he knew that the attacks were coming.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:49.300
So, we started looking for him.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:56.300
Finally, we heard that he was either in Faisalabad or in a nearby major Pakistani city.

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:59.300
Tell us how you describe Faisalabad.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:02.300
It wasn't New York City.

00:37:02.300 --> 00:37:06.300
Faisalabad was one of the most horrible places I've ever been in my life.

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:09.300
And I've been in some terrible places around the world.

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:13.300
I never felt so far away from home in my life as I did in Faisalabad.

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:19.300
It's a city of seven million people living on top of one another.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:22.300
Most of the roads are just hard-packed dirt.

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:26.300
Most of the buildings are made out of mud or concrete block.

00:37:26.300 --> 00:37:29.300
Open sewers running through the streets.

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:37.300
I tell a story in the book that when a colleague of mine or a case officer now retired

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:42.300
and I went to Faisalabad the first time just to scope the place out, maybe look for a safe house.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:47.300
We were overwhelmed by the poverty and by the stench.

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:50.300
And we realized this is going to be tough.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:51.300
We had nowhere to eat.

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:57.300
And finally in the middle of all this garbage and sewage and poverty

00:37:57.300 --> 00:38:01.300
there's a brand new McDonald's sitting right in the middle of it.

00:38:01.300 --> 00:38:08.300
So at least we had a big Mac anyway to tide us over until we got back to the office.

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:13.300
But we realized early on this was going to be a lot tougher than we had anticipated.

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:20.300
And frankly I didn't have any experience in specifically targeting a high-level al-Qaeda person.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:24.300
So we knew early on that he was moving between Faisalabad.

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.300
Well, it didn't sound like many other people.

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:28.300
No, I think not.

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:32.300
This is pretty unique operation you were involved in.

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:33.300
Right, for all of us.

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:38.300
We knew he was moving and I thought, well, I've got a great idea, I said,

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:40.300
which turned out to be a terrible idea.

00:38:40.300 --> 00:38:45.300
There's a toll road between Faisalabad and this other city.

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:48.300
We can put CIA officers in the toll booths.

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:50.300
We had a four-year-old passport photo of him.

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:52.300
And when he comes through the toll booth we grab it.

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:53.300
We grab it?

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:54.300
Well, that's a terrible idea.

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:58.300
60,000 people a day passed through that junction.

00:38:58.300 --> 00:39:02.300
So we came up with a couple of ideas that were equally bad.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:07.300
And finally I went to the senior CIA officer in the country at the time.

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:11.300
And I said, really, I think what we need is a targeting analyst.

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:15.300
I knew of a targeting analyst back home in headquarters at CTC,

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:18.300
a fantastic officer, really great at what he did.

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:26.300
And I said he can help us sort through these thousands of pages of bits of information.

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:28.300
That's what a targeting analyst does.

00:39:28.300 --> 00:39:29.300
That's what a targeting analyst does.

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:31.300
He just tries to narrow down the possibilities.

00:39:31.300 --> 00:39:32.300
Right.

00:39:32.300 --> 00:39:35.300
A targeting analyst is somebody who really has a mind for detail

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:39.300
but uses that expertise to assist in operations

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:43.300
rather than writing intelligence for policymakers.

00:39:43.300 --> 00:39:47.300
He's a relatively new discipline, new post-September 11th.

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:50.300
So he arrived in Islam about a couple of days later

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:57.300
and started by taking a large piece of paper about the size of a small U.S. billboard.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:40:03.300
And he put it up on the wall and he wrote Abu Zubedah in the middle of it with a circle around it.

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:09.300
And then around Abu Zubedah he began writing down every scrap of information we could collect,

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:13.300
whether it was a phone number or an email address or a physical address,

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:17.300
whatever we could find that we could tie to Abu Zubedah,

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:21.300
we put it around Abu Zubedah's name and put lines to Abu Zubedah.

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:25.300
And then around those, on the tertiary level, he did the same thing.

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:31.300
So there were lines connecting the tertiary with the secondary, the secondary with the primary.

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:36.300
And at the end of the week, it was really quite pretty, like a work of art.

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:39.300
It was like a spiderweb.

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:42.300
And finally he came to me and he said, I have a problem.

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:47.300
He said, I can't get this down to any fewer than 14 sites.

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:49.300
I just can't make it any smaller.

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:52.300
He could be in any one of these 14 sites.

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:57.300
Well, we had never done more than two counter-terrorist raids in a single night before.

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:58.300
And with our...

00:40:58.300 --> 00:41:00.300
And that was the object of the game.

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:04.300
To rate down to a certain number where you think there's a good chance

00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:06.300
he's going to be in one of these.

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:09.300
And then you've got to hit them all at the same time.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:10.300
That's right.

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:12.300
And hope that he's in one.

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:13.300
And hope that he's in one.

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:17.300
Now, he may be in a completely different location.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:23.300
In which case, can we then collect the information from somebody we captured in one of the 14?

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:25.300
Do we start back at square one?

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:27.300
Is he going to be spooked and then he goes deeper underground?

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:29.300
We just had no idea.

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:35.300
So I told the senior officer, we're going to need a bigger team.

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:36.300
Yeah, 14.

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:37.300
14 was a lot.

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:41.300
We decided to split it between the CIA and the FBI.

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:45.300
We brought in the Pakistani military.

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.300
They helped us greatly.

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:54.300
And we chartered a plane and brought in this team with millions of dollars in cash,

00:41:54.300 --> 00:42:00.300
with weapons and equipment and night vision goggles and secure communications.

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:03.300
You name it, we shipped it in.

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:10.300
And an Arab-American case officer and I, a wonderful heroic guy, I wish I could say his name.

00:42:10.300 --> 00:42:12.300
I call him Amir in the book.

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:17.300
Amir and I went down to Faisalabad to rent a safe house.

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:21.300
We did the same thing in this other nearby major city that I'm not permitted to name.

00:42:21.300 --> 00:42:23.300
And we got things started.

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:26.300
In the meantime, the team flew directly in.

00:42:26.300 --> 00:42:32.300
And we set up in a large hotel temporarily until we could get our new safe house in order.

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:36.300
And we started taking a closer look at these 14 sites.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:46.300
So what we decided to do was go out the day before the night of the raids

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:51.300
and look at every one of the sites just to make sure we're not driving into an ambush.

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:53.300
We're not being set up.

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:56.300
We're not making a mistake, which is equally important.

00:42:56.300 --> 00:42:58.300
And I get into that in the book as well.

00:42:58.300 --> 00:43:00.300
We did make one mistake that night.

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:04.300
And most of these places were very small one or two room huts,

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:08.300
made of mud or made of concrete block, corrugated tin roofs.

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:10.300
There wasn't much to them.

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:14.300
While we're driving around Faisalabad, the targeting officer called me

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:19.300
and said, we just got a call from a friendly intelligence service here in town.

00:43:19.300 --> 00:43:21.300
They say that they have a walk-in.

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:25.300
And a walk-in is somebody who just comes in off the street and volunteers information.

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:28.300
So you never know if the information is correct or not.

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:34.300
But you have to treat everything as though it's true and important, just in case.

00:43:34.300 --> 00:43:36.300
You have to look into everything.

00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:44.300
Well, the walk-in said that there was a large group of Arabs inside a large, distinctly painted house.

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:47.300
And so we're driving around that day.

00:43:47.300 --> 00:43:52.300
And sure enough, there's a large, brightly colored house.

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:55.300
And I said to our Pakistani escort,

00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:57.300
What's going on there?

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:01.300
And he said, I can tell you right now something bad is going on in there

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:06.300
because it was at least 100 degrees outside that day and all the shutters were closed.

00:44:06.300 --> 00:44:08.300
And it had to have been broiling inside that house.

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:11.300
We made a note to put an extra large team on that site.

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:16.300
Well, finally, we're headed to the last site.

00:44:16.300 --> 00:44:20.300
And the targeting analyst called back and said,

00:44:20.300 --> 00:44:24.300
Abu Zabeda just made a critical error.

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:29.300
He said he just accessed the internet from a landline.

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:31.300
Well, that was a critical error.

00:44:31.300 --> 00:44:34.300
So with the landline, we had a phone number.

00:44:34.300 --> 00:44:37.300
And with the phone number, presumably we could get an address.

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:39.300
So I said to the Pakistanis,

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:42.300
Can you tell us where this is located?

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:44.300
Oh, yes, they gave us an address.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:48.300
Well, the address was the final site on our list.

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:52.300
And Amir and I looked at each other and I remember just smiling and he said,

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:53.300
We got him.

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:59.300
Well, we got to the site and much to our surprise, it was just an empty field.

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:02.300
So I said to the Pakistani, how could this possibly be?

00:45:02.300 --> 00:45:06.300
We're certain that the site is a good site.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:09.300
And he said, no, this is normal in Pakistan.

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:13.300
What they do is whenever you have a plot of land,

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:16.300
there's a telephone number assigned to that plot of land.

00:45:16.300 --> 00:45:21.300
And it just, it's in anticipation of somebody building a structure on the land

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:22.300
and then you have your telephone.

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:26.300
So what happens is people will climb up the telephone pole,

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:31.300
they'll splice the wire and steal the telephone line, run it to their house.

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:35.300
So when they make a call, the bill goes to the owner of the land,

00:45:35.300 --> 00:45:37.300
not to the person who makes a call.

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:42.300
He calls a young technical officer from his service.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:46.300
The young man arrives and climbs the telephone pole.

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:49.300
He goes through this Medusa's head of wires up there.

00:45:49.300 --> 00:45:52.300
Finally, he finds a wire, he follows it down the pole

00:45:52.300 --> 00:45:56.300
and he's literally following it hand over hand down an alley.

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:59.300
And he says, it's that house right there.

00:45:59.300 --> 00:46:00.300
Well, then we knew.

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:01.300
Then you had it.

00:46:01.300 --> 00:46:02.300
We had it.

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:03.300
That's great.

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:05.300
We had the sun to go down.

00:46:05.300 --> 00:46:08.300
We split up our teams and finally we got everybody together

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:11.300
actually before we split up and got into buses.

00:46:11.300 --> 00:46:14.300
And I stood on a coffee table and I said,

00:46:14.300 --> 00:46:16.300
guys, I don't mean to sound dramatic,

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:19.300
but we're going to have to synchronize our watches.

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:20.300
And everybody chuckled.

00:46:20.300 --> 00:46:23.300
And I said, seriously, we're going to have to synchronize our watches

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:24.300
because here's the plan.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:27.300
0130, everybody heads out in the buses.

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:32.300
0145, you leave the safe houses in cars and go to the site.

00:46:32.300 --> 00:46:35.300
0155, eyes on the site.

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:39.300
And then right at 0200, I said, not a second before

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:42.300
or a second after, we break the door down.

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:45.300
Once you get inside, separate women and children from the men,

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:48.300
cuff the men, and then start going through everything.

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:49.300
FBI has the lead.

00:46:49.300 --> 00:46:51.300
They take pictures of everything.

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:52.300
They process the evidence.

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:55.300
We do the debriefings and then we can take it from there.

00:46:55.300 --> 00:46:57.300
Well, John, it was a big deal,

00:46:57.300 --> 00:47:00.300
but I want to push you a little bit now

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:03.300
because we're just getting to the meaty stuff.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:05.300
In point of fact, you did get it.

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:06.300
We did.

00:47:06.300 --> 00:47:08.300
And he was in pretty bad shape when you got him

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:09.300
because he'd been shot.

00:47:09.300 --> 00:47:10.300
Shot three times, yes.

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:13.300
So take it from there because I think we're moving

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:15.300
towards this issue of, you know,

00:47:15.300 --> 00:47:18.300
what has become a sensational case of

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:21.300
how do we get information from this individual?

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:23.300
Right, he had been shot three times in the thigh,

00:47:23.300 --> 00:47:26.300
the groin, and the stomach with an AK-47

00:47:26.300 --> 00:47:29.300
while trying to jump from the roof of his safe house

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:31.300
to the roof of the neighboring house

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:34.300
and to escape, the Pakistani military shot him.

00:47:34.300 --> 00:47:38.300
There were some extraordinary events that took place,

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:41.300
that resulted in his life being saved.

00:47:41.300 --> 00:47:45.300
Extraordinary care by Pakistani surgeons is what I mean.

00:47:45.300 --> 00:47:48.300
I'll skip ahead to the point where he finally,

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:51.300
we had to evacuate him to a military base nearby.

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:54.300
And my orders were very specific.

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:56.300
Very specific from Director Tenant,

00:47:56.300 --> 00:47:58.300
conveyed through Islamabad,

00:47:58.300 --> 00:48:02.300
24-7 CIA eyes on is what Director Tenant said,

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:05.300
which meant I had to tear up a sheet,

00:48:05.300 --> 00:48:07.300
tie him to the bed, because I didn't know

00:48:07.300 --> 00:48:09.300
if the doctor's going to help him break out

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:11.300
or the nurses, I didn't know, any of these people,

00:48:11.300 --> 00:48:13.300
tied him to the bed and I just sat there

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:15.300
and waited for him to wake up.

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:19.300
So finally, about a day later he came to,

00:48:19.300 --> 00:48:21.300
I go into detail in the book about

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:24.300
our first two conversations,

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:26.300
if you can call them conversations.

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:29.300
In the second of the two he asked me to smother him with a pillow.

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:32.300
But later on, when he finally started getting his bearings,

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:36.300
I told him, look, I'm the nicest guy

00:48:36.300 --> 00:48:39.300
you're going to encounter in this experience.

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:42.300
So I'm going to tell you, you have to cooperate.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:45.300
The rest of your life can be very difficult

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:47.300
or it can be very easy.

00:48:47.300 --> 00:48:49.300
It's up to you and you have to cooperate.

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:53.300
Now, I didn't have any idea what the agency planned for him.

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:55.300
Frankly, at the time, the agency didn't have any idea

00:48:55.300 --> 00:48:57.300
what to do with him, because we had never found ourselves

00:48:57.300 --> 00:48:59.300
in a position like this before,

00:48:59.300 --> 00:49:02.300
where we had captured a high-ranking al-Qaeda member.

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:04.300
So that policy was being debated at headquarters

00:49:04.300 --> 00:49:08.300
as we were sitting there having this conversation.

00:49:08.300 --> 00:49:11.300
He wasn't really interested in cooperating.

00:49:11.300 --> 00:49:14.300
He wanted to talk about Islam versus Christianity.

00:49:14.300 --> 00:49:16.300
He wanted to recite poetry.

00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:19.300
He would cry because he would never know the joy of fatherhood.

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:22.300
He would never know the happiness of marriage.

00:49:22.300 --> 00:49:25.300
And he was very concerned about what was going to happen to him.

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:32.300
I didn't want it to sound like it was all going to be flowers and tea

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.300
at his next destination.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.300
But again, I didn't know what was going to happen.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:39.300
And I urged him repeatedly, every time we talked,

00:49:39.300 --> 00:49:42.300
you have to cooperate, you have to cooperate.

00:49:42.300 --> 00:49:44.300
Finally, a few days later...

00:49:44.300 --> 00:49:46.300
But you reached a conclusion, which I thought was terrific.

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:53.300
You put it in the book about the value of a direct conversation like this.

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:56.300
That's what you said, an earnest, direct conversation

00:49:56.300 --> 00:49:59.300
is better than blasting him with a baseball bat

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:01.300
or all the other coercive stuff.

00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:03.300
Oh, I believe that strongly.

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:07.300
Because in the very limited time that he and I had,

00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:09.300
we actually did carry on conversations.

00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:12.300
Now, of course, I wasn't going to get the crown jewels

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:15.300
of Al-Qaeda's operational plans from him in those first few days.

00:50:15.300 --> 00:50:17.300
But I believed then...

00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:19.300
You were developing rapport.

00:50:19.300 --> 00:50:20.300
Exactly right.

00:50:20.300 --> 00:50:23.300
And I still believe that today, that if you have the luxury of time,

00:50:23.300 --> 00:50:26.300
you can develop a rapport to the point where that person

00:50:26.300 --> 00:50:30.300
is going to tell you things that he otherwise wouldn't have.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:34.300
And you make the good point of the luxury of time.

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:39.300
So, at this point, were you fairly certain

00:50:39.300 --> 00:50:41.300
that he was going to survive?

00:50:41.300 --> 00:50:42.300
Was he...?

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:43.300
No.

00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:44.300
I wasn't, actually.

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:48.300
The doctor told me that he had never seen wounds so severe

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:49.300
where the patient lived.

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:50.300
Oh, boy.

00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:53.300
And Abu Zubaydah was bleeding profusely.

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:55.300
I mean, it was pulling under the bed.

00:50:55.300 --> 00:50:58.300
It soaked him and the sheets.

00:50:58.300 --> 00:51:01.300
It was all over us every time we tried to move him.

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:02.300
He was bleeding profusely.

00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:05.300
And they could barely get the blood into him fast enough

00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:07.300
and it would just seep right out.

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:12.300
So, Director Tennant asked the agency's executive director,

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:13.300
Buzzy Kroengard at the time,

00:51:13.300 --> 00:51:17.300
who was on the board of directors of Johns Hopkins University

00:51:17.300 --> 00:51:20.300
to find a Johns Hopkins trauma surgeon

00:51:20.300 --> 00:51:24.300
who could go out and sort of take charge of Abu Zubaydah's recovery.

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:26.300
And that's exactly what happened.

00:51:26.300 --> 00:51:30.300
This doctor came on an agency plane a couple of days later.

00:51:30.300 --> 00:51:32.300
We loaded Abu Zubaydah onto the plane.

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:35.300
I told him again, you have to cooperate.

00:51:35.300 --> 00:51:39.300
He was afraid, despite the fact he had asked me to kill him

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:40.300
in that initial conversation,

00:51:40.300 --> 00:51:43.300
he was afraid that's exactly what was going to happen

00:51:43.300 --> 00:51:45.300
to the point where he was crying.

00:51:45.300 --> 00:51:46.300
And I said, no, no.

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:48.300
I said, nobody's going to do anything to you.

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:51.300
You're going to get the best medical care in the world.

00:51:51.300 --> 00:51:52.300
You're going to recover.

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:55.300
You're going to get past this, but you have to cooperate.

00:51:55.300 --> 00:51:57.300
And that was the last thing I said to him.

00:51:57.300 --> 00:52:00.300
I loaded him onto the plane, and then I never saw him again.

00:52:00.300 --> 00:52:01.300
Right.

00:52:01.300 --> 00:52:06.300
But you came to understand that his life was saved.

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:07.300
It was indeed.

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:08.300
He did survive.

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:09.300
Yes, it took about six weeks.

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:14.300
And then the questioning started in earnest.

00:52:14.300 --> 00:52:15.300
Yes.

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:17.300
And you formed a view about that.

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:18.300
I did.

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:20.300
When I got back to headquarters,

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:22.300
I was asked by a senior officer

00:52:22.300 --> 00:52:25.300
if I wanted to be what he called certified

00:52:25.300 --> 00:52:27.300
in the use of enhanced interrogation techniques.

00:52:27.300 --> 00:52:29.300
And he explained to me what that meant.

00:52:29.300 --> 00:52:31.300
Because, as you were saying, I hate to interrupt,

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:35.300
but as you said, nobody really knew

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:38.300
what the regime of questioning was going to turn out to be

00:52:38.300 --> 00:52:42.300
because we'd never captured a high-value person like this.

00:52:42.300 --> 00:52:43.300
That's right.

00:52:43.300 --> 00:52:45.300
So they were trying to decide what they were going to do,

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:46.300
and then they decided.

00:52:46.300 --> 00:52:47.300
It was an ongoing process.

00:52:47.300 --> 00:52:48.300
Right.

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:51.300
And enhanced interrogation was going to be the order of the day.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:53.300
So, excuse me, back to you.

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.300
You were asking about yourself.

00:52:56.300 --> 00:52:57.300
Right.

00:52:57.300 --> 00:52:59.300
And he told me what this meant.

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:01.300
And I had some serious doubts about it.

00:53:01.300 --> 00:53:05.300
So I sought the counsel of a very senior CIA officer

00:53:05.300 --> 00:53:07.300
who I had great respect for.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:10.300
And he said something that sticks in my mind to this day.

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:12.300
He said, first of all, let's call a spade a spade.

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:14.300
He said, it's torture.

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:16.300
They can call it enhanced interrogation techniques

00:53:16.300 --> 00:53:18.300
or whatever else they want, but it's torture.

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:21.300
Second, this is a slippery slope.

00:53:21.300 --> 00:53:23.300
Something's going to go wrong.

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:26.300
Somebody's going to do something that's illegal

00:53:26.300 --> 00:53:28.300
and somebody's going to get hurt.

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:31.300
Third, it's going to leak, either to Congress or to the press,

00:53:31.300 --> 00:53:33.300
equally bad.

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:35.300
And there's going to be a congressional investigation

00:53:35.300 --> 00:53:37.300
and then a grand jury's going to be called.

00:53:37.300 --> 00:53:39.300
And somebody's going to go to prison.

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:41.300
He said, do you want to be involved in that?

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:42.300
And I said, absolutely not.

00:53:42.300 --> 00:53:45.300
So I went back and I said, thanks for thinking of me,

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:46.300
but I'm going to decline.

00:53:46.300 --> 00:53:48.300
And another officer that I knew went through the same thing

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:50.300
and he also declined.

00:53:50.300 --> 00:53:55.300
So I kind of settled back into my next position,

00:53:55.300 --> 00:53:58.300
but I kept an eye open for what was happening to him.

00:53:58.300 --> 00:54:01.300
I was very interested in this person who you had captured

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:03.300
and basically saved.

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:04.300
That's right.

00:54:04.300 --> 00:54:06.300
And I wanted to know what was happening.

00:54:06.300 --> 00:54:09.300
And I was hoping that he was cooperative.

00:54:09.300 --> 00:54:12.300
But it was clear from the beginning when he first

00:54:12.300 --> 00:54:15.300
recovered that he wasn't going to cooperate.

00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:19.300
And so they began using these harsh interrogation techniques

00:54:19.300 --> 00:54:20.300
on him.

00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:23.300
Which had been, as we now know, taken to the office

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:26.300
of legal counsel and had been approved at that level

00:54:26.300 --> 00:54:30.300
and sent back to the agency for execution.

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:34.300
But there were aspects of this that were troublesome to you,

00:54:34.300 --> 00:54:36.300
even so.

00:54:36.300 --> 00:54:39.300
There were not every enhanced interrogation technique

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:41.300
as torture.

00:54:41.300 --> 00:54:43.300
For example, if I grab you by the lapels and say,

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:46.300
answer my questions, that's not torture in my mind

00:54:46.300 --> 00:54:48.300
if I just grab your lapels.

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:50.300
But things like waterboarding.

00:54:50.300 --> 00:54:53.300
I think everybody's familiar with waterboarding now.

00:54:53.300 --> 00:54:56.300
Or even this notion of a cold cell,

00:54:56.300 --> 00:54:58.300
where a cell is chilled to 50 degrees,

00:54:58.300 --> 00:55:01.300
the prisoner's stripped naked or near naked,

00:55:01.300 --> 00:55:03.300
and he has cold water thrown on him.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:05.300
Or sleep deprivation.

00:55:05.300 --> 00:55:10.300
I know myself 24 hours and I start to not be right.

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:14.300
And I can't imagine going five, seven, nine days

00:55:14.300 --> 00:55:16.300
without sleep.

00:55:16.300 --> 00:55:18.300
I think that's torture.

00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:20.300
I think it's wrong.

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:23.300
And then you came to understand that even with your

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:28.300
initial conclusion about what waterboarding was,

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:32.300
it wasn't just once that it was done to Zubeda.

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:34.300
I've come to a conclusion on this.

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:38.300
I told ABC News in December of 2007

00:55:38.300 --> 00:55:40.300
that Abu Zubeda was waterboarded one time

00:55:40.300 --> 00:55:42.300
and he gave actionable intelligence

00:55:42.300 --> 00:55:44.300
that disrupted a terrorist attack.

00:55:44.300 --> 00:55:47.300
I based that on the cable traffic that I was reading

00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:51.300
that was coming back from Abu Zubeda's secret site.

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.300
But the CIA Inspector General released a report,

00:55:54.300 --> 00:55:57.300
or the CIA released the Inspector General's report

00:55:57.300 --> 00:55:59.300
last year in April,

00:55:59.300 --> 00:56:02.300
in which the Inspector General, John Helgerson,

00:56:02.300 --> 00:56:04.300
concludes that the agency had been waterboarding

00:56:04.300 --> 00:56:07.300
Abu Zubeda before it had written authority to do so.

00:56:07.300 --> 00:56:09.300
They were waterboarding him in anticipation

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:11.300
of receiving this written authority.

00:56:11.300 --> 00:56:16.300
And I think that not every waterboarding session

00:56:16.300 --> 00:56:17.300
was being reported.

00:56:17.300 --> 00:56:19.300
In fact, I think they just consolidated it

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:22.300
into the one cable that I saw and said he was waterboarded.

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:24.300
Here's what he said.

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:25.300
It's actionable.

00:56:25.300 --> 00:56:28.300
Now, Director Hayden said in congressional testimony

00:56:28.300 --> 00:56:31.300
a year ago or so, two years ago,

00:56:31.300 --> 00:56:34.300
that the information was actionable

00:56:34.300 --> 00:56:37.300
and it did result in the disruption of an attack.

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:39.300
But then you have to ask yourself two questions.

00:56:39.300 --> 00:56:41.300
Number one, was it right morally?

00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:45.300
I say in the book I think it was not right.

00:56:45.300 --> 00:56:47.300
But number two, did it work?

00:56:47.300 --> 00:56:50.300
If you have to waterboard somebody 83 times,

00:56:50.300 --> 00:56:52.300
which we now know is what happened

00:56:52.300 --> 00:56:55.300
before he gives you the information,

00:56:55.300 --> 00:56:58.300
I think again it doesn't work.

00:56:58.300 --> 00:57:02.300
If that report had been developed,

00:57:03.300 --> 00:57:05.300
I think he probably would have given

00:57:05.300 --> 00:57:09.300
at least enough information to allow us to act on it.

00:57:09.300 --> 00:57:10.300
A couple of weeks ago,

00:57:10.300 --> 00:57:13.300
the Deputy Director for Operations at the CIA, Mike Sulick,

00:57:13.300 --> 00:57:16.300
gave a speech at a university in Indiana

00:57:16.300 --> 00:57:19.300
in which he said that in retrospect,

00:57:19.300 --> 00:57:22.300
the information we got from Abu Zubaydah,

00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:25.300
we could have gotten from other sources

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:29.300
or we could have gotten just through normal interrogation techniques.

00:57:29.300 --> 00:57:34.300
He said in the end he concludes that it did not save American lives.

00:57:34.300 --> 00:57:36.300
And so on a cost-benefit basis.

00:57:36.300 --> 00:57:37.300
It wasn't worth it.

00:57:37.300 --> 00:57:40.300
It wasn't worth it because of what it did.

00:57:40.300 --> 00:57:42.300
Well, we haven't got a lot of time

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:44.300
but I think let's take this narrative

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:49.300
through your succeeding jobs

00:57:49.300 --> 00:57:51.300
and what you came to conclude

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:54.300
that caused you to leave the agency.

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:57.300
Right, I was blessed with a fantastic

00:57:57.300 --> 00:58:00.300
onward assignment once I finished in Pakistan.

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:03.300
I went to work as the Executive Assistant

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:05.300
to one of the Associate Deputy Directors.

00:58:05.300 --> 00:58:07.300
Not really realizing at the time

00:58:07.300 --> 00:58:09.300
that he was named Associate Deputy Director

00:58:09.300 --> 00:58:13.300
in order to coordinate the agency's participation

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:15.300
in the planning for the Iraq War.

00:58:15.300 --> 00:58:17.300
So on my first day in that job,

00:58:17.300 --> 00:58:19.300
I was read into a compartment

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:21.300
that was shocking to me.

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:23.300
Once I signed my secrecy agreement,

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:25.300
the officer who read me and said,

00:58:25.300 --> 00:58:27.300
here's the deal, next March,

00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:29.300
and this was in August of 2002,

00:58:29.300 --> 00:58:32.300
next March we're going to invade Iraq,

00:58:32.300 --> 00:58:34.300
we're going to overthrow Saddam Hussein,

00:58:34.300 --> 00:58:37.300
and we're going to open the world's largest air force base

00:58:37.300 --> 00:58:39.300
so that we can move all of our air assets

00:58:39.300 --> 00:58:41.300
out of Saudi Arabia into Iraq

00:58:41.300 --> 00:58:43.300
and deprive Osama bin Laden

00:58:43.300 --> 00:58:45.300
of the ability to say that we're polluting

00:58:45.300 --> 00:58:47.300
the land of the two holy mosques,

00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:49.300
which is what he calls Saudi Arabia.

00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:52.300
And I was so stunned, all I could think to say was

00:58:52.300 --> 00:58:54.300
but we haven't caught bin Laden yet.

00:58:54.300 --> 00:58:56.300
And anticipating that response,

00:58:56.300 --> 00:58:58.300
he said it's a done deal,

00:58:58.300 --> 00:59:00.300
the decision's been made,

00:59:00.300 --> 00:59:02.300
we're going to invade Iraq.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:04.300
Well it turned out the Associate Deputy Director

00:59:04.300 --> 00:59:06.300
with whom I was working

00:59:06.300 --> 00:59:08.300
told me that he had been informed

00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:10.300
in March of 2002, a full year

00:59:10.300 --> 00:59:12.300
before we went into Iraq

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:14.300
that this decision had been made.

00:59:14.300 --> 00:59:16.300
The senior officer went on to tell me

00:59:16.300 --> 00:59:18.300
that we were going to go to the United Nations

00:59:18.300 --> 00:59:20.300
by the end of the year,

00:59:20.300 --> 00:59:22.300
and that we wanted an international coalition.

00:59:22.300 --> 00:59:24.300
We knew that the Chinese, the Russians

00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:26.300
and probably the French would oppose us

00:59:26.300 --> 00:59:28.300
and that we were prepared to go it alone.

00:59:28.300 --> 00:59:30.300
So we were going to wait until the spring

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:32.300
and do it in March.

00:59:32.300 --> 00:59:34.300
And that became my job for the next year

00:59:34.300 --> 00:59:36.300
was working with the agency to come up

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:38.300
with its response.

00:59:38.300 --> 00:59:40.300
And as you point out

00:59:40.300 --> 00:59:42.300
that just meant that we were taking our eye

00:59:42.300 --> 00:59:44.300
off the ball in Afghanistan

00:59:44.300 --> 00:59:46.300
and the whole effort to find

00:59:46.300 --> 00:59:48.300
Osama and we were opening up

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:50.300
another can of worms.

00:59:50.300 --> 00:59:52.300
I thought at the time it was a terrible mistake

00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:54.300
and I'll say one other thing.

00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:56.300
Inside the agency as word began to get around

00:59:56.300 --> 00:59:58.300
as we got closer to

00:59:58.300 --> 01:00:00.300
the March

01:00:00.300 --> 01:00:02.300
date and Vice President Cheney

01:00:02.300 --> 01:00:04.300
began talking publicly about

01:00:04.300 --> 01:00:06.300
some sort of connection between Saddam Hussein

01:00:06.300 --> 01:00:08.300
and

01:00:08.300 --> 01:00:10.300
Al-Qaeda

01:00:10.300 --> 01:00:12.300
we began calling it the big lie

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:14.300
at the agency.

01:00:14.300 --> 01:00:16.300
Because all of us knew there was no connection

01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:18.300
between Saddam Hussein.

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:20.300
None whatsoever.

01:00:20.300 --> 01:00:22.300
Well John, you've written a terrific book here.

01:00:22.300 --> 01:00:24.300
Thank you.

01:00:24.300 --> 01:00:26.300
We've got a little bit of time.

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:28.300
Is there anything you want to say

01:00:28.300 --> 01:00:30.300
as sort of a concluding

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:32.300
set of observations?

01:00:32.300 --> 01:00:34.300
I'd like to say that

01:00:34.300 --> 01:00:36.300
my goal in writing this book

01:00:36.300 --> 01:00:38.300
was to show the American people

01:00:38.300 --> 01:00:40.300
that they have a lot to be proud of

01:00:40.300 --> 01:00:42.300
at the CIA.

01:00:42.300 --> 01:00:44.300
You have a group of

01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:46.300
people that only want to serve the country

01:00:46.300 --> 01:00:48.300
and I think that some of the agency's

01:00:48.300 --> 01:00:50.300
success stories need to be told.

01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:52.300
I think the American people want to know

01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:54.300
that the agency is succeeding.

01:00:54.300 --> 01:00:56.300
I think they have a right to please

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:58.300
that I was able to get this cleared

01:00:58.300 --> 01:01:00.300
by the CIA and that the story is out there.

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:02.300
I hope people enjoy it.

01:01:02.300 --> 01:01:04.300
I think it's a terrific book John.

01:01:04.300 --> 01:01:10.660
Thanks for talking about it with me today.

01:01:10.660 --> 01:01:12.660
Thanks for having me.

